1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,851
An A I agent can now hold a payment credential
and complete a checkout on your behalf.

2
00:00:05,911 --> 00:00:08,939
Agentic commerce standardised how that agent
pays.

3
00:00:08,999 --> 00:00:13,756
It never gave the merchant any way to read
what the agent is allowed to spend.

4
00:00:15,016 --> 00:00:16,293
Three things follow.

5
00:00:16,353 --> 00:00:22,507
The merchant recommends and only then finds
out, so the offer trips a limit it could not see.

6
00:00:22,567 --> 00:00:32,464
The obvious fix, a cap inside the agent, holds against the agent's own mistakes and nothing
else, because a compromised agent simply does not run it.

7
00:00:32,524 --> 00:00:37,409
And bolted onto a blind merchant, every
authority check can only subtract.

8
00:00:38,669 --> 00:00:39,990
PACT closes that gap.

9
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:44,723
A human signs a spending mandate in their own
browser, on their own key.

10
00:00:44,783 --> 00:00:47,945
The agent carries that mandate and never the
key.

11
00:00:48,005 --> 00:00:53,007
And before the merchant prices anything, it
reads how much authority is left.

12
00:00:54,267 --> 00:00:58,057
The mandate is signed on the buyer's device
with Ed25519.

13
00:00:58,117 --> 00:01:04,675
The agent carries it to the merchant, which
reads the remaining headroom before it builds a quote.

14
00:01:04,735 --> 00:01:14,062
Every request then goes to the gate, which decides on authority alone, across ten checks
in a fixed order, cheapest and most certain first.

15
00:01:14,122 --> 00:01:17,506
Only an allowed decision reaches a settlement
rail.

16
00:01:18,666 --> 00:01:21,405
What the merchant reads is deliberately thin.

17
00:01:21,465 --> 00:01:27,126
Headroom remaining, purchases left, the
per-transaction ceiling, allowed categories, expiry.

18
00:01:27,186 --> 00:01:33,097
Not the buyer's identity, not their intent,
not their spend history, and never the total budget.

19
00:01:33,157 --> 00:01:38,818
A merchant that learns eight thousand nine
hundred rupees remaining learns what it can sell.

20
00:01:38,878 --> 00:01:44,602
One that learns eight thousand nine hundred of
fifteen thousand learns how rich the buyer is.

21
00:01:45,862 --> 00:01:47,750
Three things make this hold up.

22
00:01:47,810 --> 00:01:50,390
First, ceilings are reserved, not counted.

23
00:01:50,450 --> 00:01:56,737
The budget is summed over reservations inside
the same immediate transaction that writes the new row.

24
00:01:56,797 --> 00:02:02,707
The naive read-then-write version is kept in
the repository so the two can be run side by side:

25
00:02:02,767 --> 00:02:11,505
twenty concurrent agents against a five thousand rupee cap, and the naive ledger
approves twenty of them and overspends by fifteen thousand.

26
00:02:12,665 --> 00:02:18,202
Second, the gate decides authority and a rail
moves money, and neither knows the other.

27
00:02:18,262 --> 00:02:20,454
Nothing in the core imports a rail.

28
00:02:20,514 --> 00:02:25,730
A test walks the syntax tree to enforce it,
and a second test closes the loophole:

29
00:02:25,790 --> 00:02:29,205
no executable line in the core may even name a
vendor.

30
00:02:30,365 --> 00:02:32,927
Third, every offer is provably approvable.

31
00:02:32,987 --> 00:02:41,417
The test suite puts every add-on the merchant
suggests through the real gate, and a single block fails the build. That is the inversion:

32
00:02:41,477 --> 00:02:50,469
the same gate that refuses bad transactions now shapes good ones, so the spending limit
stops being friction and becomes a conversion instrument.

33
00:02:51,929 --> 00:02:53,724
This is the running system.

34
00:02:53,784 --> 00:02:56,954
The human grants authority on their own
device:

35
00:02:57,014 --> 00:03:00,940
fifteen thousand rupees, five purchases, three
categories.

36
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:13,515
The signature is produced in the browser, and the badge in the header is the console
verifying that the browser's Ed25519 and canonical J S O N agree with the server's, byte for byte.

37
00:03:14,475 --> 00:03:15,467
The agent shops.

38
00:03:15,527 --> 00:03:22,103
The merchant prices the basket server side,
the gate runs its checks in order, and the order settles.

39
00:03:22,163 --> 00:03:28,805
Every check is on screen, including the ones
that were skipped, and the whole path takes milliseconds.

40
00:03:29,665 --> 00:03:31,003
Now the product itself.

41
00:03:31,063 --> 00:03:38,111
The merchant read the remaining authority
before it made this offer, which is why the badge on it says the item fits.

42
00:03:38,171 --> 00:03:47,164
The buyer accepts, the add-on is re-quoted into the same order, and the authority bar
moves — an upsell that was approvable before it was ever shown.

43
00:03:48,024 --> 00:03:50,450
The same offer, made the obvious way.

44
00:03:50,510 --> 00:03:52,936
Recommend first, find out afterwards.

45
00:03:52,996 --> 00:03:56,362
The gate refuses it with a per-transaction
ceiling:

46
00:03:56,422 --> 00:04:00,999
a failed offer, and a buyer who now trusts
their agent a little less.

47
00:04:01,959 --> 00:04:03,485
Four attacks in sequence.

48
00:04:03,545 --> 00:04:12,689
A captured request replayed, a payee outside the mandate's scope, a price the model
invented, and an instruction hidden in a product description.

49
00:04:12,749 --> 00:04:18,148
Each one refused server side, and each with a
machine-readable code rather than prose.

50
00:04:19,008 --> 00:04:21,187
Then the failure worth planning for.

51
00:04:21,247 --> 00:04:24,609
The money has already moved and the warehouse
is empty.

52
00:04:24,669 --> 00:04:26,413
Compensations run in reverse:

53
00:04:26,473 --> 00:04:32,012
the payment is refunded, the ceiling gives the
budget back, and an alternative is offered.

54
00:04:32,072 --> 00:04:40,348
Accepting it returns a quote the buyer signs,
because the merchant holds no key and must never be able to spend on the buyer's behalf.

55
00:04:41,208 --> 00:04:43,812
Everything so far was the merchant's view.

56
00:04:43,872 --> 00:04:47,808
This is the same decision seen by the person
whose money it is:

57
00:04:47,868 --> 00:04:50,980
every check in order, and the one that stopped
it.

58
00:04:51,940 --> 00:04:57,311
Four arms, two hundred sessions each, three
seeds, run against the real services.

59
00:04:57,371 --> 00:05:06,296
Against a naive client-side cap, PACT nets about a quarter more, with a zero percent
false block rate against that cap's twenty-eight.

60
00:05:06,356 --> 00:05:15,684
Its losses to adversarial traffic are zero, and stay zero at every adversarial rate swept,
while an ungated agent channel degrades linearly.

61
00:05:16,844 --> 00:05:20,239
And the number that does not flatter us,
reported anyway.

62
00:05:20,299 --> 00:05:26,845
Below roughly eighteen percent adversarial
traffic, an ungated channel still nets more under this loss model.

63
00:05:26,905 --> 00:05:33,633
The baseline arm is modelled rather than
simulated, and the payment client has never run against the live A P I.

64
00:05:33,693 --> 00:05:37,875
All three are stated in the results the
harness generates, not buried.

65
00:05:39,235 --> 00:05:39,489
PACT.

66
00:05:39,549 --> 00:05:48,163
A signed delegation the agent carries, a gate that decides on authority alone, and a
merchant that only ever offers what will be approved.
